"Internal Error ()\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2007.12.003\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2007.12.003: (500) Redirected to https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S004873330700248X which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.techfore.2006.03.002\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://doi.org/10.1016/j.techfore.2006.03.002: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enpol.2014.01.043\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enpol.2014.01.043: (500) Redirected to https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0301421514000822 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1039/C8EE01157E\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://doi.org/10.1039/C8EE01157E is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1038/nclimate3231\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://doi.org/10.1038/nclimate3231 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExploring possible transition pathways for hydrogen energy: A hybrid approach using socio-technical scenarios and energy system modelling (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1447670/1/1-s2.0-S0016328714001232-main.pdf)\nciteturn6view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1447670/1/1-s2.0-S0016328714001232-main.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 789\nL0@P0: Exploring possible transition pathways for hydrogen energy:\nL1@P0: A hybrid approach using socio-technical scenarios and energy\nL2@P0: system modelling\nL3@P0: Will McDowall a,b,\nL4@P0: *\nL5@P0: aUCL Energy Institute, United Kingdom bUCL Institute of Sustainable Resources, United Kingdom\nL6@P0: 1. Introduction\nL7@P0: Hydrogen remains an important option for long-term decarbonisation of energy and transport systems, and modelling\nL8@P0: studies often suggest that hydrogen could be an important part of an affordable and achievable transition to a low carbon\nL9@P0: economy. Despite a recent period of disappointmentfollowing several years of hydrogen ‘hype’ (Bakker, 2010),technological\nL10@P0: progress in hydrogen technologies has been promising. Automotive firms have focused on vehicles running on pure\nL11@P0: hydrogen with fuel cells, and on-board compressed hydrogen, moving away from earlier work with liquid hydrogen or on\u0002board conversion of other fuels. Costs have fallen, and there is increasing confidence from automakers that fuel cell vehicles\nL12@P0: are approaching commercial competitiveness.\nL13@P0: However, studying the possible transition paths and development prospects for a hydrogen energy system is challenging.\nL14@P0: The long-term nature of technological transitions inevitably means profound uncertainties, diverging perspectives and\nL15@P0: Futures 63 (2014) 1–14\nL16@P0: A R T I C L E I N F O\nL17@P0: Article history:\nL18@P0: Available online 24 July 2014\nL19@P0: Keywords:\nL20@P0: Socio-technical scenarios\nL21@P0: Energy system modelling\nL22@P0: Hydrogen energy\nL23@P0: Exploratory scenarios\nL24@P0: A B S T R A C T\nL25@P0: Hydrogen remains an important option for long-term decarbonisation of energy and\nL26@P0: transport systems. However, studying the possible transition paths and development\nL27@P0: prospects for a hydrogen energy system is challenging. The long-term nature of\nL28@P0: technological transitions inevitably means profound uncertainties, diverging perspectives\nL29@P0: and contested priorities. Both modelling approaches and narrative storyline scenarios are\nL30@P0: widely used to explore the possible future of hydrogen energy, but each approach has\nL31@P0: shortcomings.\nL32@P0: This paper presents a hybrid approach to assessing hydrogen transitions in the UK, by\nL33@P0: confronting qualitative socio-technical scenarios with quantitative energy systems\nL34@P0: modelling, through a process of ‘dialogue’ between scenario and model. Three possible\nL35@P0: transition pathways are explored, each exploring different uncertainties and possible\nL36@P0: decision points. Conclusions are drawn for both the future of hydrogen, and on the value of\nL37@P0: an approach that brings quantitative formal models and narrative scenario techniques into\nL38@P0: dialogue.\nL39@P0: \u0002 2014 The Author. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY\nL40@P0: license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/).\nL41@P0: * Correspondence to: UCL Energy Institute, 14 Upper Woburn Place, London WC1H 0NN, United Kingdom. Tel.: +44 20 3108 5992.\nL42@P0: E-mail address: w.mcdowall@ucl.ac.uk\nL43@P0: Contents lists available at ScienceDirect\nL44@P0: Futures\nL45@P0: journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/futures\nL46@P0: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.futures.2014.07.004\nL47@P0: 0016-3287/\u0002 2014 The Author. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/\nL48@P0-1: 3.0/).contested priorities. Both modelling approaches and narrative storyline scenarios are widely used to explore the possible\nL49@P1: future of hydrogen energy, but each approach has shortcomings.\nL50@P1: This paper presents a hybrid approach to examining hydrogen transitions in the UK, by linking qualitative transition\nL51@P1: scenarios with quantitative energy systems modelling. The approach acknowledges the contested nature of ways\nL52@P1: of understanding future possibilities by placing two different methods (participatory storylines and energy\nL53@P1: systems modelling) in explicit ‘dialogue’. Three possible transition pathways are explored, each exploring different\nL54@P1: uncertainties and possible decision points, with modelling used to inform and test key elements of each scenario. The\nL55@P1: scenarios draw on literature review and participatory input, and the scenario structure is based on patterns identified in\nL56@P1: historical energy system transitions, reflecting insights relating to innovation system development and resistance to\nL57@P1: change.\nL58@P1: 2. Background and approach: scenarios and models for technology transitions\nL59@P1: 2.1. Modelling energy transitions\nL60@P1: Formal models are powerful ways of exploring the dynamics of systems and hence play a crucial role in thinking about\nL61@P1: how those systems might develop in the future. A wide variety of models have been developed to inform the transition to\nL62@P1: a low carbon economy, and these have generated robust1 insights into the likely importance and roles of various\nL63@P1: technologies, trends and policy instruments. In the context of hydrogen energy, three types of models2 have been\nL64@P1: prominent:\nL65@P1: \u0002 So-called ‘‘Bottom-up’’ energy system models (e.g. MARKAL and MESSAGE) evaluate the desirability of hydrogen within\nL66@P1: the context of overall decarbonisation. They model trade-offs with the wider energy system, and so provide greater\nL67@P1: techno-economic consistency than sectoral approaches, but they have weak spatial representation, and many have\nL68@P1: simplistic representations of technology dynamics and the economy-wide costs of energy transitions (Barreto & Kemp,\nL69@P1: 2008; Hourcade, Jaccard, Bataille, & Ghersi, 2006). Examples of studies addressing hydrogen transitions using such models\nL70@P1: include (Barreto, Makihira, & Riahi, 2003; Endo, 2007; Gu¨l, Kypreos, Turton, & Barreto, 2009; Krzyzanowski, Kypreos, &\nL71@P1: Barreto, 2008; Mau, Eyzaguirre, Jaccard, Collins-Dodd, & Tiedemann, 2008; Strachan, Balta-Ozkan, Joffe, McGeevor, &\nL72@P1: Hughes, 2009; Yeh, Farrell, Plevin, Sanstad, & Weyant, 2008).\nL73@P1: \u0002 System dynamics and agent-based simulation models examine interactions between agents (governments, consumers, car\nL74@P1: manufacturers). These models are valuable in showing how simple relationships can result in complex dynamics similar to\nL75@P1: previous attempts to foster alternative fuel transitions; and they can provide insights into the conditions under which\nL76@P1: heterogeneous actors might foster a transition through consumption, investment, policy and cooperation decisions.\nL77@P1: However, they lack the broader system view, without feedbacks and synergies between sectors in the wider economy.\nL78@P1: Examples in the field of hydrogen transitions include (Contestabile, 2010; Hue´tink, der Vooren, & Alkemade, 2010; Keles,\nL79@P1: Wietschel, Mo¨ st, & Rentz, 2008; Ko¨hler, Wietschel, Whitmarsh, Keles, & Schade, 2010; Schwoon, 2008; Struben & Sterman,\nL80@P1: 2008).\nL81@P1: \u0002 Infrastructure optimisation transition models. These optimise spatial and temporal aspects of infrastructure and vehicle\nL82@P1: deployment, but exogenise hydrogen demand. For a review, see (Agnolucci & McDowall, 2013).\nL83@P1: Quantitative models used in the analysis of possible transitions have grown increasingly sophisticated, endogenising the\nL84@P1: effects of scale economies and learning (Schwoon, 2008), social network effects (Hue´tink et al., 2010; Mau et al., 2008), and\nL85@P1: strategic games between actors (Schlecht, 2003). Energy systems models have been adapted to incorporate better\nL86@P1: representation of behaviour (Daly et al., 2012; Mau et al., 2008), macro-economic developments (Strachan & Kannan, 2008);\nL87@P1: and technological change (Anandarajah, McDowall & Ekins, 2013).\nL88@P1: However, on their own, none of these model types is able to provide a compelling account of transition dynamics, since in\nL89@P1: the real world the structure of the system itself evolves. In other words, the rules guiding development co-evolve with\nL90@P1: technologies, user behaviours and business strategies (Foxon, 2011). Moreover, there is scant agreement on the extent to\nL91@P1: which dominant rule structures used in models provide a good approximation of socio-technical developments over long\nL92@P1: time periods (Trutnevyte, 2014). As a result, existing models may be unable to represent the key issues that are widely\nL93@P1: recognised by stakeholders to be important. These issues then lie outside the scope of any formal analysis, potentially\nL94@P1: remaining unexamined tacit assumptions that guide decisions. Attempts to develop models of transitions dynamics that are\nL95@P1: informed by evolutionary and co-evolutionary thinking are developing, but are still in their infancy (Safarzyn´ ska, Frenken, &\nL96@P1: van den Bergh, 2012).\nL97@P1: 1 At least, robust in the face of the uncertainties that are considered to be most well characterised, following Lempert and Groves definition of ‘robustness’\nL98@P1: of model outcomes (Groves and Lempert, 2007). D.G. Groves, R.J. Lempert, A new analytic method for finding policy-relevant scenarios, Global\nL99@P1: Environmental Change, 17 (2007) 73–85. 2 Others have also been applied, such as Computable General Equilibrium models, but these have been less frequently used.\nL100@P1-2: 2 W. McDowall / Futures 63 (2014) 1–142.2. Socio-technical scenarios\nL101@P2: Scenarios3 are widely used to help inform decision-making in the face of significant uncertainty, particularly in fields with\nL102@P2: long-term planning horizons such as energy policy. A major reason for adopting an exploratory scenario approach as an\nL103@P2: analytic tool for considering possible energy decarbonisation transition paths is a belief that formal quantitative models are\nL104@P2: unable to adequately represent the dynamics of socio-technical change, for the reasons discussed above (So¨derholm,\nL105@P2: Hildingsson, Johansson, Khan, & Wilhelmsson, 2011; Swart, Raskin, & Robinson, 2004). Rather than ignore the issues that are\nL106@P2: already informing stakeholder decisions because they are not tractable in a formal model, scenario approaches draw these\nL107@P2: out, make them explicit, and conduct thought experiments to test judgements about their importance.\nL108@P2: Scenario storylines informed by participatory processes, though not always as analytically coherent or internally\nL109@P2: consistent in techno-economic terms, are thus able to capture, distill and explore ideas about the future that are currently\nL110@P2: shaping stakeholder perceptions, but that cannot be adequately represented in formal modelling frameworks. The\nL111@P2: resulting scenarios do not incorporate the technical rigour of models, but they can be valuable in making explicit widely\nL112@P2: held views about possible technology dynamics. This does not necessarily mean that these are more ‘accurate’ in terms of\nL113@P2: predicting what kinds of dynamics are likely. Indeed, that is not the core aim. Scenarios are ‘learning machines’ (Berkhout,\nL114@P2: Hertin, & Jordan, 2002) that can enable reflection on the realism or implications of widely held views, and on how\nL115@P2: stakeholders understand and relate to different possibilities. Rather than provide evidence to inform concrete decisions,\nL116@P2: such scenarios foster ‘conceptual learning’, i.e. providing new insights, perspectives and ideas on policy issues, a function\nL117@P2: seen as very important within the literature on the use of evidence in policymaking (Hertin, Turnpenny, Nilsson, Russel,\nL118@P2: & Nykvist, 2009).\nL119@P2: Recent years have seen the development of scenario approaches designed specifically to inform understanding of possible\nL120@P2: technological transitions—shifts from one dominant socio-technical system to another (archetypal examples being the shift\nL121@P2: from sailing ships to steam ships, or from gas lighting to electric lighting). Informed by the burgeoning literature on\nL122@P2: technologicaltransitions (Markard, Raven, & Truffer, 2012), such scenario approaches attemptto reflect understanding ofthe\nL123@P2: dynamics of technological change, focusing in particular on the relative durability of different institutional and socio\u0002technical configurations, and the co-evolutionary dynamics of technologies, users and institutions (Elzen, Geels, & Hofman,\nL124@P2: 2002; Elzen, Geels, Hofman, & Green, 2004; chap. 11; Foxon, Hammond, & Pearson, 2010). In the arena of hydrogen energy,\nL125@P2: there have been several attempts to develop qualitative socio-technical scenarios inspired by transitions research to\nL126@P2: examine potential hydrogen transitions (Eames & McDowall, 2010; Van Bree, Verbong, & Kramer, 2010).\nL127@P2: 2.2.1. The UKSHEC II scenarios approach\nL128@P2: This project goes beyond those previous socio-technical hydrogen scenarios by developing qualitative scenarios in\nL129@P2: parallel with modelling work. Quantitative modelling has been used in combination with scenario planning since the origins\nL130@P2: of the field (Wack, 1985). One common approach is the use of scenario storylines as tools for identifying and differentiating\nL131@P2: the values of key parameters for modelling exercises, with the resulting dynamics of change still determined by the model\nL132@P2: (e.g. Barreto et al., 2003). A second common alternative is the detailed quantification of narrative scenarios, to ensure that\nL133@P2: they are technically feasible and consistent (for example, Dutton et al., 2004).\nL134@P2: Others have highlighted the way in which the complementary strengths of qualitative storyline scenarios and\nL135@P2: quantitative modelling tools can be put to good use by comparing and contrasting the insights and dynamics produced in\nL136@P2: each method (Alcamo, 2008, chap. 6; Ault, Frame, Hughes, & Strachan, 2008; Fontela, 2000), often using multiple iterations\nL137@P2: between modelling and scenario writing. Alcamo describes this as the ‘SAS’ (storyline and simulation) approach (Alcamo,\nL138@P2: 2008), and describes its use by the IPCC and others. In the energy field, examples of work of this kind include (Ault et al.,\nL139@P2: 2008) and (Fortes, Alvarenga, Seixas, & Rodrigues, 2014), both of whom use energy system models to explore qualitative\nL140@P2: scenarios developed through participatory stakeholder processes. Recent work within the UK’s Realising Transition\nL141@P2: Pathways project has also linked models to qualitative socio-technical transition scenarios, through quantification of\nL142@P2: storylines and iteration with various modelling tools (Foxon, 2013).\nL143@P2: The UKSHEC II project follows in that tradition, though with a looser coupling of model runs and scenario storylines\nL144@P2: than is typically undertaken. In this project, socio-technical scenarios and energy system modelling have been used in\nL145@P2: parallel. The model is not forced to reproduce the dynamics of each storyline, and model runs are not to be understood as\nL146@P2: quantified versions of the storylines. Instead, modelling exercises are used to examine and inform elements of the\nL147@P2: scenarios, while the scenarios are used to challenge and confront the results suggested by the model. This can be\nL148@P2: described as a ‘dialogue’ between the two approaches, rather than a process of using one to provide input into the other,\nL149@P2: and with no attempt to arrive at fully quantified model-based equivalents to the qualitative storylines. The approach has\nL150@P2: similarities with approaches based on ‘constructive conflict’ in stakeholder dialogue (Cuppen, 2010), which attempt to\nL151@P2: confront different stakeholder positions, and thereby promote ‘‘an open exploration and evaluation of competing ideas\nL152@P2: 3 There is frequently confusion about the purpose and utility of scenario approaches, in part due to the great diversity of applications, which arise from\nL153@P2: the fact that the future is profoundly uncertain and that not thinking about or making assumptions about the future is impossible. Confusion also arises\nL154@P2: because most models are run different with sets of input parameters, for which the term scenario is typically used. That model-specific use of the term\nL155@P2: scenario is distinct from what are here termed ‘exploratory scenarios’, which develop qualitative, narrative storylines of alternative possible futures.\nL156@P2-3: W. McDowall / Futures 63 (2014) 1–14 3and knowledge claims in order to achieve new ideas [and] new insights. . .’’ (Cuppen, 2010, p. 26). Here, the approach\nL157@P3: confronts two contrasting ‘‘worldviews’’, one derived from stakeholder opinion, the other a model that operates as a\nL158@P3: planner optimising the energy system.\nL159@P3: 3. Developing socio-technical storylines: methods and approach\nL160@P3: The methodological approach used in this study followed a simple sequences of stages, similar to many other socio\u0002technical scenario development exercises. The method draws on that suggested by Hughes (2013). Note that many of these\nL161@P3: stages are overlapping and iterative.\nL162@P3: 1. Development of theoretical framework for describing transitions.\nL163@P3: 2. Participatory involvement of expert stakeholders to scope key issues, uncertainties and possible dynamics.\nL164@P3: 3. ‘Mapping’ the system in terms of actors, regime structure, niches, and landscape developments, and identification of key\nL165@P3: strategic uncertainties and the branching points that they imply.\nL166@P3: 4. Writing of storylines, with a structure drawn from insights from transitions research, attempting to highlight key\nL167@P3: branching points and their possible implications.\nL168@P3: 5. ‘‘Dialogue’’ with modelling: use scenarios to identify issues that may not be addressed with models, and use models to\nL169@P3: highlight potential weaknesses in the scenarios.\nL170@P3: 3.1. Step 1: developing a theoretical framework for describing possible transitions\nL171@P3: Two complementary and related theoretical frameworks, drawn from the technological transitions literature, are used to\nL172@P3: structure the analysis of the key uncertainties and the way in which they may unfold. This framework is briefly described\nL173@P3: here.\nL174@P3: First, the analysis is situated within the multi-level perspective (MLP) on technological transitions (Geels, 2002), and\nL175@P3: draws on the typology of Geels and Schot in order to inform some basic transition ‘types’ (Geels & Schot, 2007). Their\nL176@P3: typology is based on two dimensions:\nL177@P3: i. The timing of interactions (how mature is the niche when the regime comes under pressure).\nL178@P3: ii. Nature of interaction (relationship of the niche innovation to the broader regime, i.e. is the niche innovation disruptive or\nL179@P3: re-enforcing to existing regime).\nL180@P3: Beliefs about the status of hydrogen with regard to these dimensions differ. Geels and Schot offer four criteria for\nL181@P3: determining whether the niche innovation is mature:(a)the presence of a dominant design,(b) presence of powerful actors\nL182@P3: in the innovation system supporting the technology, (c) price/performance have improved and there are expectations of\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"An Economic and Technological Analysis of the Key Factors Affecting th\" by Wei Feng and Miguel A. Figliozzi (https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/cengin_fac/258/)\nciteturn6view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/cengin_fac/258/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 62\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Search】\nL2:   * 【2†Browse Collections】\nL3:   * 【3†My Account】\nL4:   * 【4†About】\nL5:   * 【5†Digital Commons Network™†network.bepress.com】\nL6: \nL7: 【6†Skip to main content】\nL8: \nL9: 【0†Home】 > 【7†School, College, or Department】 > 【8†MCECS】 > 【9†CEE】 > 【10†Faculty Publications】 > 【11†258】\nL10: \nL11: ## 【10†Civil and Environmental Engineering Faculty Publications and Presentations】\nL12: # An Economic and Technological Analysis of the Key Factors Affecting the Competitiveness of Electric Commercial Vehicles: A Case Study from the USA Market\nL13: \nL14:   * 【12†Wei Feng, Portland State University】\nL15:   * 【13†Miguel A. Figliozzi, Portland State University】【14†Follow†network.bepress.com】\nL16: \nL17: ## Sponsor\nL18: \nL19: The authors would like to acknowledge the support of Oregon Transportation Research and Education Consortium (OTREC) for supporting this research\nL20: ## Published In\nL21: \nL22: Transportation Research Part C: Emerging Technologies\nL23: \nL24: ## Document Type\nL25: \nL26: Citation\nL27: \nL28: ## Publication Date\nL29: \nL30: 2013\nL31: \nL32: ## Subjects\nL33: \nL34: Commercial vehicles, Electric vehicles, Vehicle replacement, Economic analysis\nL35: ## Abstract\nL36: Electric commercial vehicles’ (ECVs) energy costs are almost four times less expensive than conventional diesel trucks, on a per-mile basis, at current USA market values. However, ECVs are approximately three times more expensive in terms of vehicle purchase costs. In addition, electric vehicles are simpler and cheaper to maintain there are more uncertainties associated to the life and long-term costs of the ECV batteries.\nL37: Furthermore, there are limitations in terms of miles driven per day without recharging. These economic and technological tradeoffs motivate this research. Utilizing a fleet replacement optimization framework, a wide range of scenarios, and current USA market data this research finds the key economic and technological breakeven values where ECVs become competitive against conventional diesel counterparts.\nL38: The results clearly indicate that only in scenarios with high utilization (over 16,000 miles per year per truck) the electric vehicles are competitive, this is especially valid if a battery replacement is not required before the electric commercial vehicle is replaced. The breakeven analysis results show that a 9–27% ECV price reduction can greatly increase their competitiveness when vehicles are driven over 12,000 miles per year.\nL39: ## Rights\nL40: \nL41: Copyright 2013 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.\nL42: \nL43: ## Locate the Document\nL44: \nL45: 【15†https://doi.org/10.1016/j.trc.2012.06.007†doi.org】\nL46: \nL47: ## DOI\nL48: \nL49: 10.1016/j.trc.2012.06.007\nL50: \nL51: ## Persistent Identifier\nL52: \nL53: http://archives.pdx.edu/ds/psu/20810\nL54: ## Citation Details\nL55: \nL56: Feng, Wei and Figliozzi, Miguel A., \"An Economic and Technological Analysis of the Key Factors Affecting the Competitiveness of Electric Commercial Vehicles: A Case Study from the USA Market\" (2013). Civil and Environmental Engineering Faculty Publications and Presentations. 258.\nL57: http://archives.pdx.edu/ds/psu/20810\nL58: \nL59: ## Share\nL60: \nL61: COinS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nelectronic library - A new vehicle for urban freight? An ex-ante evaluation of electric cargo bikes in courier services (https://elib.dlr.de/88873/)\nciteturn6view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://elib.dlr.de/88873/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 80\nL0: 【0†Image: DLR-Header】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†DLR Portal†www.dlr.de】  | 【2†Home】 | 【3†Imprint】 | 【4†Privacy Policy】 | 【5†Accessibility】 | 【6†Contact】 | 【7†Deutsch】  |\nL3:  |  | Fontsize: [-] Text [+]\nL4: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6:   * 【8†Advanced Search】\nL7:  | 【9†Image】Tell a friend 【10†Image】Print\nL8: # A new vehicle for urban freight? An ex-ante evaluation of electric cargo bikes in courier services\nL9: [Input][Select][Input] Gruber, Johannes and Kihm, Alexander and Lenz, Barbara (2014) A new vehicle for urban freight? An ex-ante evaluation of electric cargo bikes in courier services. Research in Transportation Business & Management. Elsevier. doi: 【11†10.1016/j.rtbm.2014.03.004†doi.org】. ISSN 2210-5395.\nL10: 【12†PDF】\nL11: 1MB\nL12: Official URL: 【13†http://authors.elsevier.com/sd/article/S2210539514000091†authors.elsevier.com】\nL13: ## Abstract\nL14: One possible strategy to tackle the negative effects of urban freight is the substitution of cars by electric cargo bikes for inner-city courier shipments. This paper determines whether there is a potential market for electric cargo bikes, how the current market is organized, how electric cargo bikes are perceived by bike and car messengers, and what factors drive their willingness to use them.\nL15: We find that in terms of cost, payload and range, electric cargo bikes lie in between two existing modes (bikes and cars) that have a largely overlapping market. Vehicle choice is commonly made by freelance messengers, as many courier companies don't operate their own fleets. Therefore they can contribute only indirectly to the dissemination of electric cargo bikes by considering them in their operational management.\nL16: Despite the fact that most messengers have not used an electric cargo bike before, it was generally regarded to be suitable for courier shipments. Using a binary logit model, we find that messengers' demographics, their professional practice as well as their attitudes and values have significant impacts on their willingness to use electric cargo bikes. Critical factors for actual implementation appear to be electric range, purchase price and publically available information.\nL17: Item URL in elib: 【14†https://elib.dlr.de/88873/】\nL18: Document Type: Article\nL19: Title: A new vehicle for urban freight? An ex-ante evaluation of electric cargo bikes in courier services\nL20: Authors:\nL21: Authors Institution or Email of Authors Author's ORCID iD ORCID Put Code\nL22: Gruber, Johannes Johannes.Gruber (at) dlr.de 【15†https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5537-5473†orcid.org】 UNSPECIFIED\nL23: Kihm, Alexander alexander.kihm (at) dlr.de UNSPECIFIED UNSPECIFIED\nL24: Lenz, Barbara barbara.lenz (at) dlr.de UNSPECIFIED UNSPECIFIED\nL25: Date: 13 April 2014\nL26: Journal or Publication Title: Research in Transportation Business & Management\nL27: Refereed publication: Yes\nL28: Open Access: Yes\nL29: Gold Open Access: No\nL30: In SCOPUS: Yes\nL31: In ISI Web of Science: Yes\nL32: DOI: 【11†10.1016/j.rtbm.2014.03.004†doi.org】\nL33: Editors:\nL34: Editors Email Editor's ORCID iD ORCID Put Code\nL35: Brooks, M.R. Dalhousie University, Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada UNSPECIFIED UNSPECIFIED\nL36: Ison, S. Loughborough University, Leicestershire, UK UNSPECIFIED UNSPECIFIED\nL37: Publisher: Elsevier\nL38: ISSN: 2210-5395\nL39: Status: Published\nL40: Keywords: Courier service; Electric cargo bike; User acceptance; Binary logit model\nL41: HGF - Research field: Aeronautics, Space and Transport\nL42: HGF - Program: Transport\nL43: HGF - Program Themes: Transport System\nL44: DLR - Research area: Transport\nL45: DLR - Program: V VS - Verkehrssystem\nL46: DLR - Research theme (Project): V - Projekt Verkehrsentwicklung und Umwelt (old)\nL47: Location: Berlin-Adlershof\nL48: Institutes and Institutions: 【16†Institute of Transport Research > Commercial Transport】\nL49: Deposited By: Gruber, Dr. Johannes\nL50: Deposited On: 17 Apr 2014 10:53\nL51: Last Modified: 2